#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119626 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f892805c-2c2c-5105-91ba-353aefe7b7f2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of the Space Spanned by a Set of Vectors Using Inspection"}] :question/id #uuid "51c289e1-f118-5444-bf1d-c7e8262c549f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4816" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ![](images/39/39c738068392d3e8f34d72cb46d270e480067d9db8b0353ba1eb05f3a961030c.svg) and ![](images/53/53b09ea4cefbe48a6e1690bc772e74bd59f361bfbc6410a1ae02ac3555233b23.svg) Find a basis of $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3 \\}$ by inspection." :question/worked-solution "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that $\\mathbf{a}_2$ is a linear combination of vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}_2=1\\cdot \\mathbf{a}_1+1\\cdot \\mathbf{a}_3\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector $\\mathbf{a}_2$ can be removed from $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3 \\}$ without changing the span. So $S = \\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3 \\}$ is spanned by just $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_3\\}.$ \n\nLastly, note that $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_3$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_3\\}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_3\\},$ that is to say,\n\n![](images/ca/ca8ec71c9d54982494530c4458f3a596a3b61a0529acd42c9ddae3fe9c500e34.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119672 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119857 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f644bed-7adb-5907-87b4-99b1902e7850" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of the Space Spanned by a Set of Vectors by Identifying Pivot Columns"}] :question/id #uuid "f9601132-0450-51d2-8559-e83120f5fa2f" :question/math-academy-id "e-6237" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ![](images/7d/7d5c2f2aaa1ee01159856bcfad7ef1039c169c2fdb96c9c4ab17db5ba9be9067.svg) ![](images/78/784f5d018101808c55cf63b332ae50c9d4fc8e3ded23ad9144e417052d2eaba3.svg) and ![](images/49/491d6bb66fd54ae64e406eef6607526c23c6451a3ed0df837d63170c0b9adb95.svg) Find a basis of $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3 \\}.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find a subset of linearly independent vectors. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}5 & 10 & 15 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $3$rd columns are pivot columns, while the $2$nd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one.\n\nThe $1$st and $3$rd columns from the original matrix are $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_3.$ So, a basis of $S$ is $\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_3 \\},$ that is to say, \n\n![](images/fd/fd0c93483a1a4386dd0269ac11cbe99567176e1e9d5dbd53477e46cb0704074c.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119673 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119858 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b72ed196-a73b-59ab-a7c3-5173249cf7e5" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of a Span"}] :question/id #uuid "5bdbcb40-c8d4-5191-bbcd-1b066e660f68" :question/math-academy-id "e-6257" :question/problem "The vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1,$ $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ $\\mathbf{a}_3,$ and $\\mathbf{a}_4$ are given below. Determine whether $\\mathcal{B} = \\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3 \\}$ is a basis of $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3, \\mathbf{a}_4 \\}.$ If that is the case, find $x_1 + x_2 + x_3,$ where $\\mathbf{a}_4=x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2+x_3\\mathbf{a}_3.$\n![](images/3f/3ff69817bd8aeb5d8326b7ea9fdc68ceeef52840d4f77ee7dffb9f72514f4532.svg)" :question/worked-solution "We want to determine if $\\mathcal{B}$ is indeed a basis of $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3,\\mathbf{a}_4 \\}.$ So, we want to find $x_1, x_2, x_3 \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that \n![](images/79/79525b86cbbbc191f9a19ffa817fdaebeb0d1f6bd99eb97ce3f642a7fdf6033e.svg)\n\nThis equation is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=2 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=-2 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the augmented matrix of the system to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 0 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the reduced matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd columns. So,  $\\mathcal{B}$ is indeed a basis of $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3,\\mathbf{a}_4 \\}.$\nAlso, the matrix tells us that $x_1=-5,$ $x_2=0,$ and $x_3=7,$ meaning that\n![](images/c7/c732a274f27d98231f9a94dad40ff15f53e82f00b076ba46464ac640c2765703.svg)\n\nTherefore, $x_1 + x_2 +  x_3 = (-5) + 0 + 7 = 2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386585 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119857 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f644bed-7adb-5907-87b4-99b1902e7850" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of the Space Spanned by a Set of Vectors by Identifying Pivot Columns"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2764e265-a9e8-5a46-82cd-5cdf0cd96f2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386587} {:answer/id #uuid "6822ecff-44ce-5eda-b453-d6ca98c79d39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -16\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386588} {:answer/id #uuid "54b50858-6bf1-5d35-9f45-9c4958fd025f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -16\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386589} {:answer/id #uuid "e238bd6f-87f6-5cf8-b884-eeef42cb1896" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386590} {:answer/id #uuid "23232860-1422-5f4d-9e0c-af51a68e6e94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386591}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2764e265-a9e8-5a46-82cd-5cdf0cd96f2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386587} :answer-field/id #uuid "17aaf56d-891f-5d77-bb67-315f843fe760" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386586}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "500f0d4b-f4d9-5169-9a06-c653e34353e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-110855" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}],$ ${a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],$ and ${a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -16\\end{aligned}].$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find a subset of linearly independent vectors. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 8 & 3 & -16\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=-\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 8 & 3 & -16\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-8){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 11 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns, while the $3$rd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one.\n\nThe $1$st and $2$nd columns from the original matrix are ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}.$ So, a basis of $S$ is ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317510 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119857 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f644bed-7adb-5907-87b4-99b1902e7850" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of the Space Spanned by a Set of Vectors by Identifying Pivot Columns"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bf09390-0935-56eb-bfeb-02365617f6a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315186} {:answer/id #uuid "23cfbcca-d1c9-580f-a79e-cf9a925d6dae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315318} {:answer/id #uuid "349c9561-d31b-5f9d-a460-92b9a04107fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315427} {:answer/id #uuid "68996dc8-994a-508f-abd6-0dee8012e4da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315708} {:answer/id #uuid "abafb8b0-46f8-52a2-a1f9-c40457af62e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316086}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "349c9561-d31b-5f9d-a460-92b9a04107fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315427} :answer-field/id #uuid "484b2020-65a7-5604-96a1-0c5659cbed3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316712}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "585db78b-56e0-58b0-a767-e41e96247676" :question/math-academy-id "q-34456" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and ${a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find a subset of linearly independent vectors. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $3$rd columns are pivot columns, while the $2$nd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nThe $1$st and $3$rd columns from the original matrix are ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{3}.$ So, a basis of $S$ is ${{a}_{1},{a}_{3}},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409635 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f892805c-2c2c-5105-91ba-353aefe7b7f2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of the Space Spanned by a Set of Vectors Using Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9829215-b974-58e4-8c44-69d894b68143" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409623} {:answer/id #uuid "c0494c3d-162e-5780-b464-32deeb834c29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409625} {:answer/id #uuid "cbf16f8a-df5e-5944-aca2-961b097f9609" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409626} {:answer/id #uuid "dec9dd50-c1ab-549a-ab1c-6389d8a6d6c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409627} {:answer/id #uuid "f2d36c18-ea31-5abe-9c23-a6729a95f2b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409628}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2d36c18-ea31-5abe-9c23-a6729a95f2b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409628} :answer-field/id #uuid "71a4b4a1-6fc1-55b1-954d-d7c91a2836db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409633}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "60af0127-6c17-5939-bada-d5e4db0c560b" :question/math-academy-id "q-74970" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ and ${a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Find a basis of $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ by inspection." :question/worked-solution "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that ${a}_{2}$ is a linear combination of vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=1\\cdot {a}_{1}+1\\cdot {a}_{3}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector ${a}_{2}$ can be removed from $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ without changing the span. So $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is spanned by just ${{a}_{1},{a}_{3}}.$ \n\nLastly, note that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{3}}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is ${{a}_{1},{a}_{3}},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB={{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}},{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345006 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f892805c-2c2c-5105-91ba-353aefe7b7f2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of the Space Spanned by a Set of Vectors Using Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "499ee1f6-801f-57f7-aab7-474f9d374084" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342844} {:answer/id #uuid "7f5bfdd1-ee64-5fd7-9c47-3b4c4e1a2370" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343334} {:answer/id #uuid "89079bb4-c6f6-5b1d-be0d-68fe5c1822de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343425} {:answer/id #uuid "b1061a87-039d-51d8-ba91-e57582fc2d4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343791} {:answer/id #uuid "de0e2b5b-97e2-5bfc-aeeb-9c68939e0ca7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344186}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89079bb4-c6f6-5b1d-be0d-68fe5c1822de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343425} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3d7b3da-d4d8-5800-9823-61684cbd00d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344919}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3a38bfe9-93f0-569e-b890-07afd16179a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-74972" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],$ and ${a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].$ Find a basis of $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$  by inspection." :question/worked-solution "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that ${a}_{2}$ is a linear combination of vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=2\\cdot {a}_{1}+0\\cdot {a}_{3}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector ${a}_{2}$ can be removed from $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ without changing the span. So $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is spanned by just ${{a}_{1},{a}_{3}}.$ \n\nLastly, note that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{3}}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is ${{a}_{1},{a}_{3}},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB={{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}},{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409636 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119858 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b72ed196-a73b-59ab-a7c3-5173249cf7e5" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of a Span"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d2fc535-0009-527e-8e16-197236ba5926" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409616} {:answer/id #uuid "3a46ae99-d148-5f19-94ba-416fdd09e06f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409617} {:answer/id #uuid "8e0cb693-c1d6-5a53-a944-ae0f4704df17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409620} {:answer/id #uuid "b0634bb2-64a1-5d50-8b4f-34a9ef1a9df0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409622} {:answer/id #uuid "bc6282a4-c487-5b60-9f1f-e810cbeda5e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409624}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc6282a4-c487-5b60-9f1f-e810cbeda5e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409624} :answer-field/id #uuid "065dd387-4e97-5fab-8aff-b38d1bbdcf22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409631}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c63eedb8-35fe-5b46-b979-91cc24ab9428" :question/math-academy-id "q-76329" :question/problem "The vectors ${a}_{1},$ ${a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are given below. Determine whether $B={{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}.$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ where ${a}_{3}={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We want to determine if $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}.$ So, we want to find ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+{x}_{2}{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}={{[\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThis equation is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=14 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the augmented matrix of the system to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 14 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 7 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the RREF matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So,  $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}.$\nAlso, the matrix tells us that ${x}_{1}=2$ and ${x}_{2}=-5,$ meaning that\n\n$$\n2\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+(-5)\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}={{[\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2\\cdot (-5)=-10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409634 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119858 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b72ed196-a73b-59ab-a7c3-5173249cf7e5" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of a Span"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5aaf6733-e757-5737-a6d7-d37f344e2387" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409618} {:answer/id #uuid "6536472f-623b-557c-ba85-a0b19e98cf60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409619} {:answer/id #uuid "a2ba4765-28ed-5cc7-822f-5b6db17d805d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3},{a}_{4}}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409621} {:answer/id #uuid "f568bc4f-3c69-5e10-af38-07fcd1985ab1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409629} {:answer/id #uuid "fed87abb-5218-5d71-9132-58acdc6b2500" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409630}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f568bc4f-3c69-5e10-af38-07fcd1985ab1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409629} :answer-field/id #uuid "55d04c6b-2631-5950-abad-871e8e80ece8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409632}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "06d652ec-bdc3-5e47-8845-0d191af934f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-76334" :question/problem "The vectors ${a}_{1},$ ${a}_{2},$ ${a}_{3},$ and ${a}_{4}$ are given below. Determine whether $B={{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3},{a}_{4}}.$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3},$ where ${a}_{4}={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}.$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{4}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -22\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We want to determine if $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3},{a}_{4}}.$ So, we want to find ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+{x}_{2}{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}+{x}_{3}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}={{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -22\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{4}}.\n$$\n\nThis equation is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=-4 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=4 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}=-22\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the augmented matrix of the system to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 5 & -22\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 5 & -22\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 1 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+4{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the reduced matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd columns. So,  $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3},{a}_{4}}.$\nAlso, the matrix tells us that ${x}_{1}=2,$ ${x}_{2}=0,$ and ${x}_{3}=-6,$ meaning that\n\n$$\n2\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+0\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}+(-6)\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}={{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -22\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{4}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=2+0+(-6)=-4.$"}]}
